			"contents": "\nKYLER\n410 - DEC\n400 - NOV\n400 - OCT\n400 - SEP\n400 - APR\n780 - MAR\n----------\n2,490\n\n400*12 = 4,800\n\n\n61.14 --> Wayne State\n50.00 --> Rochester Christian University\n480.00 --> Gleaners\n$7,922.00\n$7,922.00 + 61.14 + 50 + 480 + 2,490\n------------------\n\n11,003.14\n\n1) What happens when the the DB representation of Manifold AI also has embedding vectors for each word?\n2) Install:\nAdaper Hub is on Github. Install adapterhub\nLoad a copy from github all-MiniLM-L6-v2\nPyTorch has to be installed /tmp folder\n\n\n\"embeddings\": {\n  \"sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2.en\": [0.23, 0.87, ...],\n  \"nomic-ai/nomic-embed-text-v1.5.en\": [0.41, 0.12, ...]\n}\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n**The math a model uses to classify Semantic vs Fuse(keyword) search — step by step:**\n\nWhen you send a query to an LLM for intent classification, here's what happens internally:\n\n**Step 1 — Tokenization.** Your query `\"read excel files\"` becomes token IDs, e.g. `[1, 1949, 23308, 2489, 2]`. Each token maps to a row in the embedding matrix.\n\n**Step 2 — Forward pass through transformer layers.** Each layer runs:\n```\nAttention(Q,K,V) = softmax(QKᵀ / √dₖ) · V\nFFN(x) = max(0, xW₁ + b₁)W₂ + b₂\n```\nAfter N layers you have a hidden state vector `h ∈ ℝᵈ` (e.g. d=768 for small models).\n\n**Step 3 — Classification head.** A linear layer maps `h → logits ∈ ℝ^C` where C=3 (fuse, semantic, hybrid). Softmax gives probabilities. This is where an AdapterHub adapter lives — it inserts a small `ΔW` bottleneck (rank 4–16) between the frozen base model weights. You only train ~0.1% of parameters.\n\n**Step 4 — What features it learns.** With training examples, it learns that:\n- Operator characters (`!`, `^`, `'`) → near-certain Fuse\n- Question structure + verb phrases → semantic\n- Short identifiers → Fuse\n- Multi-word noun phrases → semantic/hybrid\n\n**On vector databases:** A vector DB stores pre-computed embedding vectors for each extension description. At query time you embed the query, then do approximate nearest-neighbor search (HNSW or IVF) by cosine similarity. The score is `cos(q, dᵢ) = (q·dᵢ)/(|q||dᵢ|)` — this produces much better-spread scores because it operates in high-dimensional space (384–1536 dims) rather than sparse term overlap. DuckDB itself has `vss` extension for this.\n\nNow the improved widget with multi-dimensional scoring, score tooltips, and a dimension breakdown:**Hover any score badge to see the full dimension breakdown.** Here's what each dimension measures:\n\n**BM25 ×40%** — term frequency × inverse document frequency, with field weighting (name hits worth 2.5×, topic hits 1.5×, desc 1×). This is the industry standard for text retrieval. It naturally separates strong matches from weak ones because IDF heavily penalizes common words.\n\n**Edit distance ×20%** — Levenshtein similarity between query tokens and name tokens. This catches typos, prefix matches, and name fragments that TF-IDF misses entirely.\n\n**Jaccard ×20%** — token set overlap between query and description. Measures breadth of conceptual overlap rather than term frequency.\n\n**Topic affinity ×12%** — each topic has a vocabulary list; query coverage of that vocabulary scores this dimension. \"machine learning vector\" lights up the `ai` topic regardless of exact term matches.\n\n**Exact ×8%** — binary bonus for name substring matches. Small weight but breaks ties cleanly.\n\n---\n\n**On the vector DB question:** Yes — and it's the right long-term move. The `vss` extension in DuckDB itself uses HNSW internally. The pattern would be:\n\n1. Pre-compute embeddings for all extension descriptions using a small model (e.g. `all-MiniLM-L6-v2`, 384 dims, ~23MB)\n2. Store in DuckDB as a `FLOAT[384]` column with a VSS index\n3. At query time: embed the query → `SELECT name, array_cosine_similarity(embedding, $query_vec) AS score FROM extensions ORDER BY score DESC LIMIT 10`\n\nCosine similarity in 384 dimensions produces dramatically better score spread because vectors that are genuinely dissimilar end up far apart in high-dimensional space — a property that sparse term overlap simply cannot achieve.\n\n---\n\n**On the LLM router with AdapterHub:**\n\nThe structured exchange would look like:\n\n```json\n// Request to the model\n{\n  \"query\": \"read excel spreadsheet files\",\n  \"context\": { \"corpus\": \"duckdb_extensions\", \"modes\": [\"fuse\",\"semantic\",\"hybrid\"] }\n}\n\n// Model response\n{\n  \"mode\": \"semantic\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.87,\n  \"reasoning\": \"Multi-word noun phrase with domain concept 'excel/spreadsheet'. No structural operators. Concept-verb 'read' present.\",\n  \"fuse_options\": null,\n  \"signals\": [\"concept-verb\", \"domain-concept\", \"multi-word\"]\n}\n```\n\nThe adapter makes this viable at small scale. You freeze a base model like `distilbert-base-uncased` (66M params), then train a **LoRA adapter** (rank 8 = ~200K trainable params, 0.3% of total) on ~500 labeled examples of `{query → mode}` pairs. The adapter inserts `ΔW = BA` (two low-rank matrices) into the attention layers — at inference you compute `W₀x + BAx` instead of `W₀x`. AdapterHub hosts pre-trained adapters; you'd look for one trained on query intent classification and fine-tune the final classification head on your three classes. Inference runs on CPU in ~15ms for a model this size, making it viable as a parallel call while the main search runs.\n",
